---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "just ingredients protein powder",
      "just ingredients protein",
      "just ingredients whey",
      "just ingredients review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Just Ingredients Protein Powder: Clean Label, Fair Price?</Fragment>
      A grass-fed whey blend with pea, chia and collagen, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit and sold at Whole Foods, Sprouts and Target. Here is what the label delivers, which flavor to buy and who should skip it.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Powder</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Lineup</a>
      <a href="#collagen">The Collagen Question</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which Flavor to Buy</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Heavy Metals</a>
      <a href="#who-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#compare">How It Compares</a>
      <a href="#use">Using It Well</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Just Ingredients makes a short-label, naturally sweetened whey blend that tastes like a treat to many buyers, but some of its 22 g of protein is collagen, it carries more saturated fat than a typical whey, and it costs a premium. It is a good fit for clean-label shoppers who want a creamy shake, not the most efficient protein you can buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients is a wellness brand founded by Karalynne Call, whom the company describes as a certified nutritionist and podcast host. It built its audience online with an ingredient-first message and then moved into retail: its protein powders arrived at select Whole Foods Market stores in 2024 and at Sprouts Farmers Market locations nationwide in 2025, per the company's press releases, and the brand also sells at Target.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein itself is a multi-source blend. The vanilla flavor lists about 22 g of protein in a 33 g scoop from grass-fed, non-denatured whey, pea protein, grass-fed collagen and chia seed protein, with coconut milk for creaminess, sweetened with stevia leaf and monk fruit, in roughly 140 calories, per the Whole Foods and Amazon listings at the time of writing. The brand says it uses no artificial sweeteners, sugar alcohols or gums.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a genuinely short, readable label, and buyers often praise the taste. The trade-offs are less obvious. Collagen is counted in the protein total but is a lower-quality protein for muscle, the coconut milk adds around 4.5 g of saturated fat per scoop, and the price per 25 g of protein is high. None of those are deal breakers, but they matter if you are buying protein for training rather than as a daily wellness shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict: Just Ingredients is a good choice for clean-label shoppers who want a creamy, naturally sweetened shake and do not mind paying for it. Lifters on a budget and anyone chasing maximum protein quality per scoop should look at the straight wheys in our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A tasty, short-label blend that trades efficiency for creaminess</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">About 22 g of protein from whey, pea, collagen and chia, 140 calories, stevia and monk fruit, no gums. Good for clean-label daily shakes. Skip it if you want the most muscle-building protein per dollar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is Actually in the Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per the vanilla label at the time of writing, one 33 g scoop gives about 22 g of protein, 140 calories, 6 g of fat with 4.5 g of it saturated, 5 g of carbohydrate including 2 g of fiber and 2 g of sugar, 40 mg of cholesterol and 170 mg of sodium. Each tub holds 20 servings. The brand's own materials describe 22 to 24 g of protein per serving across flavors, so check the panel on the flavor you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The whey is described as grass-fed and non-denatured, and the company says it is sourced from New Zealand. Pea, chia and collagen round out the vanilla flavor, and the chocolate version lists pumpkin seed protein as well. Whey is the backbone here. It is the highest-quality protein in the blend, rich in leucine, the amino acid most associated with triggering muscle protein synthesis.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fat number is the one most buyers do not expect. At about 6 g per scoop, Just Ingredients carries several times the fat of a typical whey isolate, mostly from the coconut milk powder, and most of that fat is saturated. It is what makes the shake creamy, and in a balanced diet it is not a problem, but if you are watching saturated fat for heart health, it is worth counting.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners are stevia leaf and monk fruit, with vanilla extract, vanilla bean and cinnamon for flavor in the vanilla version, and the brand says there are no artificial sweeteners, sugar alcohols or gums. That makes it one of the cleaner sweetener systems on the market. The labels list dairy and coconut as allergens, so it is not suitable for anyone with a milk allergy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label figure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla (per scoop)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Includes collagen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher than a bare whey</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 g, 4.5 g saturated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">From coconut milk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia and monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No artificial sweeteners</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Just Ingredients Protein Lineup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients sells one protein formula in several flavors, so the cards below are the flavors we could confirm on Amazon at the time of writing, followed by two clearly labeled alternatives from other brands. Figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Collagen Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides are a selling point on the front of the tub, and the brand markets them for skin, joint and connective tissue support. For muscle, though, collagen is a weak protein. It is missing tryptophan, an essential amino acid, and it is low in leucine, so the grams of protein that come from collagen do less for muscle building than the same grams from whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients does not disclose how much of each scoop's protein comes from each source, so we cannot say exactly how many of the 22 g are collagen. What we can say is that the effective muscle-building dose per scoop is somewhat lower than the headline number suggests. That is true of every protein blend with collagen in it, not just this one.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many buyers this does not matter. If you are using a shake for convenience, satiety or general nutrition, and you get plenty of protein from food, the blend is fine. If you are trying to maximize muscle gain, a straight whey gives you more leucine per gram and per dollar. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powder</a> guide covers collagen as its own category if the skin and joint angle is what interests you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical fix is to dose up slightly. If you want an effective 25 g serving of high-quality protein after training, a scoop of Just Ingredients mixed with a cup of milk, which adds roughly 8 g of complete protein, gets you there. Or pair the shake with a whole-food protein such as Greek yogurt or eggs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Flavor to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients sells one core protein formula in several flavors rather than a range of different products. On Amazon at the time of writing we could confirm Vanilla, Chocolate and Strawberry Cream listings, each a 20-serving tub at about 22 g of protein per scoop. Retail launches have also included flavors such as Creamy Peanut Butter Chocolate, Mint Chocolate and French Vanilla, depending on the store.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vanilla is the safest first buy. It is the most versatile flavor for smoothies, oats and baking, and the vanilla bean and cinnamon give it a more natural profile than most vanilla wheys. If you mainly blend your protein with fruit, vanilla is the right choice, and our <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">best vanilla protein powder</a> guide compares it with other options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate is the pick if you drink your protein on its own or with milk. Be aware that chocolate flavors of any protein tend to test higher for heavy metals than vanilla, because cacao absorbs lead and cadmium from soil. That is not unique to Just Ingredients, but it is worth knowing if you drink a shake every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strawberry Cream is the outlier and the one to try only if you like fruit-flavored shakes. Buyer feedback on fruit flavors is more divided than on vanilla or chocolate, so buy a single tub before stocking up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the roughly $50 list price we saw for a 20-serving tub, a scoop of Just Ingredients costs about $2.50. Because a scoop holds about 22 g of protein, that works out to roughly $2.85 per 25 g of protein. Prices move with promotions and retailers, so treat that as an approximate figure and check the current listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            That puts Just Ingredients near the top of the market. Single-ingredient grass-fed wheys such as Naked Whey usually cost noticeably less per 25 g, and certified isolates such as Thorne's often land in a similar or lower range while carrying an NSF Certified for Sport seal. You are paying a premium for the multi-source blend, the coconut creaminess and the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some of the premium is invisible on the label. Because part of each scoop is collagen, the cost per 25 g of complete, leucine-rich protein is higher still than the headline figure, so the value case rests mostly on taste and ingredient philosophy. That is a legitimate reason to buy something, but it is a reason to be clear about.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the taste but want to stretch the tub, mix half a scoop of Just Ingredients with half a scoop of an unflavored whey. You keep most of the flavor and sweetness and cut the cost per shake substantially. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide lists bases that work for this.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (approximate)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">22 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">140</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories, vanilla</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.85</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per tub</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Heavy Metals
          </h2>

          <p>
            Just Ingredients says its products are third-party tested and that each product is tested for heavy metals and other contaminants. We did not find an NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project certification for its protein at the time of writing, and we did not find published lot-by-lot results we could read.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also outside testing to weigh. Tamara Rubin, a lead-poisoning prevention advocate who commissions her own lab tests, published a 2024 report saying a Just Ingredients chocolate protein powder sample tested positive for lead, cadmium and arsenic. Her site also reports a later test of the Vanilla Bean flavor. Rubin is an advocate rather than a neutral lab, and detecting a metal is not the same as finding an unsafe level, but the report is public and worth knowing about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Context matters here. Heavy metals turn up in many protein powders, especially chocolate flavors and plant-protein blends, because cacao, pea and seed proteins pick up metals from soil. A Clean Label Project study reported that a large share of the proteins it tested exceeded at least one state or federal guideline. Dairy proteins such as whey generally test lower than plant proteins, so a blend that mixes the two will tend to land in between.
          </p>

          <p>
            If heavy metals are your top concern, choose vanilla over chocolate, ask the brand for recent certificates of analysis, or pick a product with a named certification. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide explains what the certifications test, and pregnant buyers should check any protein powder with their doctor or midwife first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Just Ingredients
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are training for muscle on a budget. You are paying about $2.85 per 25 g of protein for a blend that includes collagen, which does less for muscle than whey. A straight whey or whey isolate gives you more leucine per gram and more protein per dollar, which is what matters for strength and size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are vegan or have a milk allergy, because whey is the main protein. Skip it if you are allergic to coconut, which the labels list. And skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because we could not confirm NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, or count it carefully, if you are limiting saturated fat. At about 4.5 g of saturated fat per scoop, two shakes a day add up to a meaningful share of the daily limit many guidelines suggest. Anyone with heart disease, high cholesterol or a doctor's advice on fat intake should factor that in.
          </p>

          <p>
            As always, if you are pregnant, have kidney disease, take medication or have a medical condition, check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein powder. This review is a label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How It Compares With Other Clean-Label Proteins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against Naked Whey, the comparison is about philosophy. Naked Whey has one ingredient, grass-fed whey concentrate, with about 25 g of protein in roughly 120 calories per its label, and no sweetener at all. It is cheaper per 25 g and higher in protein quality, but you have to flavor it yourself. Just Ingredients is the ready-to-drink, sweetened version of the clean-label idea. Our sibling <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition protein powder review</a> covers that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Thorne Whey Protein Isolate, Thorne wins on verification. It carries NSF Certified for Sport status, per the brand and NSF at the time of writing, with about 21 g of protein per scoop from whey isolate. It is less creamy and less dessert-like, but it is the one to buy if you want documented testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Truvani, another clean-label brand with a short ingredient list, Just Ingredients has the edge on protein quality because of its whey base, while Truvani's flagship is plant-based and USDA Organic. Our <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani protein powder review</a> looks at that brand in detail.
