        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Using Just Ingredients Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            The creamy texture is the product's best feature, so use it where creaminess helps. Blended with frozen banana, ice and almond milk, the vanilla makes a thick, milkshake-style drink without added sugar. Stirred into overnight oats or Greek yogurt, it adds sweetness and protein in one step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because there are no gums, the powder may settle more than a gum-thickened protein, so shake or blend well and drink it soon after mixing. That is a small price for a shorter ingredient list, but it is worth knowing if you are used to shakes that stay thick in the fridge. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide has more blending ideas.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using it after training, aim for a total of around 25 to 40 g of high-quality protein in that meal. Because some of each scoop is collagen, pair it with milk or a whole-food protein rather than treating one scoop with water as a complete recovery serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, count it as food. At about 140 calories and 6 g of fat, a Just Ingredients shake is closer to a light snack than a bare protein drink. That is useful for satiety, but on a cut it should replace something rather than sit on top of your usual meals. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps you set the daily total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting all 22 g as muscle-building protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some of each scoop is collagen, which lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine. The headline number overstates the muscle-building dose. After training, mix with milk or pair the shake with a whole-food protein so you get a full 25 g or more of complete protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking the saturated fat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coconut milk gives the shake its creaminess, and about 4.5 g of saturated fat per scoop. One shake is fine for most people, but two or three a day add up. If your doctor has told you to limit saturated fat, count it like you would any other food.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming clean label means certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Just Ingredients says it tests every product, but we could not confirm NSF, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project certification. A short ingredient list says nothing about contaminants. If verified testing matters, ask for recent certificates of analysis or choose a certified product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing chocolate for daily heavy-metal peace of mind</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate proteins from every brand tend to test higher for lead and cadmium, because cacao absorbs them from soil. A public advocate's test found metals in a Just Ingredients chocolate sample. If you drink a shake every day and worry about metals, vanilla is the lower-risk flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per tub, not per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20-serving tub at about $50 looks similar to many wheys, but 22 g per scoop and 20 servings make it roughly $2.85 per 25 g. Compare every protein on cost per 25 g, and you will see where the premium sits before you subscribe.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Just Ingredients protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a good-tasting, short-label protein. The vanilla gives about 22 g of protein from grass-fed whey, pea, collagen and chia in roughly 140 calories, sweetened with stevia and monk fruit, per the label. Its drawbacks are collagen in the protein total, about 4.5 g of saturated fat and a high cost per 25 g of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Just Ingredients protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 22 g per 33 g scoop in the vanilla flavor, per the label at the time of writing, and the brand cites 22 to 24 g across flavors. Part of that protein comes from collagen, which is less effective for muscle building than whey. Each tub holds about 20 servings, so check the panel on your flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Just Ingredients protein whey or plant-based?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a blend led by grass-fed whey, with pea, chia and collagen, and pumpkin seed in the chocolate flavor. That makes it unsuitable for vegans and anyone with a milk allergy. The brand describes the whey as non-denatured and sourced from New Zealand. Dairy-free buyers should choose a plant protein instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Just Ingredients use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Stevia leaf and monk fruit, with no added sugar, per the label at the time of writing. The brand says it uses no artificial sweeteners, sugar alcohols or gums. The vanilla flavor lists about 2 g of sugar per scoop, which comes from the ingredients rather than added sugar. Flavors may differ slightly, so check your tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Just Ingredients protein have heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The brand says each product is tested for heavy metals. An independent advocate published a 2024 lab report saying a chocolate sample tested positive for lead, cadmium and arsenic. Detection is not the same as an unsafe level, and chocolate proteins generally test higher. Choose vanilla, and ask your doctor if you are pregnant.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Just Ingredients protein worth the price?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only if you value the short label and taste. At about $50 for 20 servings, it costs roughly $2.85 per 25 g of protein, which is near the top of the market. Single-ingredient wheys cost less and deliver more complete protein per gram. Mixing half a scoop with unflavored whey cuts the cost per shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where can I buy Just Ingredients protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Just Ingredients sells through its own site, Amazon, Whole Foods Market, Sprouts Farmers Market and Target, per the brand's announcements and retailer listings. Flavors vary by store. On Amazon we confirmed Vanilla, Chocolate and Strawberry Cream at the time of writing, each in a 20-serving tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
